RAWMSTAT.CONF(5)

# NAME

rawmstat.conf - rawmstat runtime configuration

# DESCRIPTION

*rawmstat*(1) uses libconfig syntax.  Configuration describes the status protocol and the blocks that produce semantic
status segments. Presentation policy such as separators and colors belongs to
*rawm*(1).

The built-in configuration contains one clock block and is usable without a
configuration file.  In normal mode configuration is layered in this order:

. built-in defaults
. _${SYSCONFDIR}/rawm/rawmstat.conf_ (normally _/etc/rawm/rawmstat.conf_)
. _$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rawm/rawmstat.conf_, when *XDG_CONFIG_HOME* is absolute;
  otherwise _$HOME/.config/rawm/rawmstat.conf_

Later scalar settings override earlier values.  When a *blocks* list is
present it replaces the complete block list from previous layers.

With *rawmstat -c path*, the system and user files are skipped: built-in
defaults are followed by exactly _path_.  A missing explicitly selected file
is an error.  Missing normal system or user files are not errors.

Unknown settings, wrong types, duplicate block names, unsupported protocols,
invalid signal offsets, and invalid UTF-8 configuration strings are fatal
configuration errors.  Use *rawmstat -C* to validate the effective
configuration without opening X or executing commands.

Libconfig *@include* directives are supported.  Relative include paths are
resolved relative to the directory of the configuration file being loaded.

# STATUS

The top-level *status* group selects the output protocol.

*protocol* = "rawm-v2"
	Output protocol. *"rawm-v2"* is the only supported value and the built-in
	default.

Example:

```
status = {
  protocol = "rawm-v2";
};
```

## rawm-v2

rawmstat publishes the *\_RAWM\_STATUS\_V2* property of the X root window
with type *UTF8_STRING* and format 8. The complete property may contain at most
4096 bytes. It consists of ordered records:

```
id<TAB>state<TAB>text<LF>
```

The block *name* becomes _id_. _state_ is *normal*, *warning*, or *critical*.
_text_ is the committed block text after optional semantic-state parsing and
configured prefix application. Empty blocks are omitted. Every emitted record,
including the last, ends in newline.

rawmstat does not choose colors or separators. *rawm*(1) maps semantic state to
its own palette and inserts its configured separator when rendering.

# BLOCKS

The top-level *blocks* setting is a list of groups. Blocks retain list order in
the rawm-v2 payload. Empty block results are omitted.

```
blocks = (
  {
    name = "clock";
    prefix = "";
    command = ("date", "+%a %b %d %H:%M");
    interval = 5;
    signal = 0;
    timeout_ms = 2000;
  }
);
```

Each block accepts these settings:

*name* = string
	Required unique segment identifier. It is transmitted as the rawm-v2 _id_
	field and must contain 1..64 characters from *A-Z*, *a-z*, *0-9*, period,
	underscore, or hyphen. Duplicate names are errors.

*prefix* = string
	Optional printable UTF-8 prepended to the committed segment text after any
	semantic state prefix is consumed. Default is the empty string. ASCII control
	characters are not permitted.

*command* = (string, ...)
	Required non-empty argument vector.  Element zero names the executable;
	the remaining elements are passed as its arguments.  rawmstat calls
	*execvp*(3) directly.  Shell parsing, expansion, redirection, pipelines,
	and globbing do not occur unless a shell is explicitly named in the vector.

*interval* = integer
	Periodic update interval in seconds.  Must be >= 0.  Zero disables periodic
	updates; the block is still executed once at startup and may be refreshed by
	its configured signal.  Scheduling uses a monotonic clock.

*signal* = integer
	Realtime-signal offset.  Must be >= 0 and available on the host.  Zero
	disables signal-triggered updates.  A value _N_ maps to *SIGRTMIN+N*.
	Multiple blocks may intentionally use the same non-zero offset and will all
	be refreshed by that signal.

*timeout_ms* = integer
	Maximum lifetime of one command invocation in milliseconds.  The value must
	be in the range 1..600000.  The built-in default is 2000.  A timed-out
	command and every process that it created in the same process group are
	terminated; the block retains its last successful value.

# COMMAND OUTPUT

rawmstat starts blocks asynchronously and captures their standard output
without blocking unrelated blocks, timers, update signals, or shutdown. Only
one invocation of a block may run at a time. If another timer or update signal
arrives while that block is running, rawmstat records one pending refresh; any
additional requests coalesce into that same pending refresh.

Only the first output line is used; subsequent output is discarded. A trailing
carriage return is removed. Successful plain output is a *normal* sample. A
block may select semantic state by beginning its first line with exactly one of:

```
normal<TAB>text
warning<TAB>text
critical<TAB>text
```

When one of those prefixes is recognized, it is removed before *prefix* is
prepended. The resulting text must be non-empty printable UTF-8 and may not
contain ASCII control characters. A successful empty first line, or a semantic
state prefix followed by empty text, clears the block.

A tab that is not part of a recognized leading state prefix makes the sample
invalid. This keeps the rawm-v2 wire grammar unambiguous without introducing an
escaping language.

A sample is committed only when the command exits normally with status 0 and
its captured text satisfies the protocol constraints. A non-zero exit, signal
termination, invalid output, read error, or timeout leaves both the previous
text and previous semantic state unchanged.

Each invocation runs in a rawmstat-owned process group. When the invocation
finishes, times out, or rawmstat shuts down, descendants in that group are not
allowed to escape as background jobs. Standard error is not captured and
remains attached to rawmstat's standard error.

The complete sequence of rawm-v2 records must not exceed 4096 bytes. If it
does, rawmstat reports an error rather than publishing a truncated message.

# EXAMPLES

A clock and load-average status:

```
status = {
  protocol = "rawm-v2";
};

blocks = (
  {
    name = "load";
    prefix = "load ";
    command = ("cut", "-d", " ", "-f", "1-3", "/proc/loadavg");
    interval = 5;
  },
  {
    name = "clock";
    command = ("date", "+%H:%M");
    interval = 30;
  }
);
```

A helper can report severity without knowing rawm's colors:

```
blocks = (
  {
    name = "cpu";
    command = ("/usr/local/bin/rawmstat-cpu");
    interval = 2;
  }
);
```

For example, that helper may print *warning<TAB>cpu:63%* or
*critical<TAB>cpu:94%*. rawmstat transmits the semantic state; rawm decides how
warning and critical states look.

A command that deliberately requires shell syntax:

```
blocks = (
  {
    name = "home";
    command = ("/bin/sh", "-c", "printf '%s' \"$HOME\"");
    interval = 0;
    signal = 1;
  }
);
```

Refresh that block with the platform's numeric value for *SIGRTMIN+1*, for
example from C via *kill*(2) or from a shell that supports realtime signal
names/numbers.

# FILES

_/etc/rawm/rawmstat.conf_
	System configuration with the default build-time *SYSCONFDIR*.

_$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rawm/rawmstat.conf_
	Preferred user configuration when *XDG_CONFIG_HOME* is absolute.

_$HOME/.config/rawm/rawmstat.conf_
	Fallback user configuration.

# SEE ALSO

*rawmstat*(1), *rawm*(1), *rawm.conf*(5), *libconfig*(5)

